; TWO ACCUMULATORS THAT READ EACH OTHER -- the shape that makes rule R's
; liveness condition load-bearing.
;
; Each of `a` and `b` is read TWICE at the back edge -- once by its own update
; and once by the other's -- so NEITHER storage is free and neither update may
; be written in place. Every backend emits the back edge as a simultaneous
; assignment whose right-hand sides are evaluated in order, so a mutation in an
; earlier one IS visible to a later one, and `a := a*b` followed by
; `b := b + a` would add the NEW a.
;
; Both updates also read the OTHER loop variable, which is what keeps them in
; the loop body: the Go emitter hoists an update into the post clause only when
; it reads nothing but its own variable, and a hoisted update runs after the
; body, where the hazard cannot arise. That is why `power` and the obvious
; two-accumulator shapes do NOT expose this -- the scheduling saves them, and a
; test that relies on being saved is testing the scheduler.
;
; It is here rather than only in a unit test because the failure is a WRONG
; ANSWER and not a compile error, and this repository's rule is that a check
; which cannot be made to fail proves nothing. With condition (3) removed this
; program returns a different number, and `gauntlet/go/bigrep_test.go` says
; which one.
;

(export pair)

(sig pair ((n (int 0 20))) (int 0 +inf))

(def pair (fn (n)
  (loop ((a 2) (b 1) (i 0))
    (>= i n)  b
    else      (again (* a b) (+ b a) (+ i 1)))))
